<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy

    What Binged collects, and why. Required for App Store submission.

    NSPrivacyTrackingDomains is deliberately empty and should stay that way:
    there is no tracking, no advertising identifier and no third-party SDK of
    any kind.

    NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes declares exactly one API, and the list is easy to
    get wrong by omission — a required-reason API used anywhere in the app must
    appear here even if it holds nothing sensitive. What the app still does NOT
    touch: file timestamps (CatalogCache stamps its own payload rather than
    asking the filesystem), disk space, boot time, and active keyboards.

    Everything under NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes is collected only for a
    signed-in user, and covers the whole of a synced library rather than only
    the published part — see docs/PART-IV-SYNC.md §5. **Signed out, nothing
    leaves the device.** That last sentence is the one to re-check whenever sync
    changes; it was cited to PART-III-SOCIAL.md §8 until Part IV superseded that
    section, and a stale citation inside a compliance artifact is worse than one
    in a design doc.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
    <false/>

    <key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
    <array/>

    <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
    <array>
        <!-- PublishConsent stores the user's answer to the publish question in
             UserDefaults, keyed by account id. CA92.1 is the reason code for
             reading and writing values accessible only to the app itself, which
             is what this is: the answer never leaves the device and is not
             shared with an app group or another process.

             The refresh token is deliberately NOT here — KeychainTokenStore
             exists so it does not live in a plist. -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
            <array>
                <string>CA92.1</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
    </array>

    <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
    <array>
        <!-- Sign-up credential. Linked to the account by definition. -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeEmailAddress</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
            <true/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
            <false/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
            <array>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
            </array>
        </dict>

        <!-- The handle: how other people find and address an account. -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeUserID</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
            <true/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
            <false/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
            <array>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
            </array>
        </dict>

        <!-- Optional display name shown on posts and profiles. -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeName</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
            <true/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
            <false/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
            <array>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
            </array>
        </dict>

        <!-- The ranked library, reviews and comments.

             NOT only the *published* part, which is what this comment said
             until 2026-08-26 and what Part III made true. Part IV syncs every
             watched, ranked item with an externalId the moment a user is
             signed in, published or not, because that is what restores a
             library onto a second device. `is_published` decides who else may
             read a row; it has never decided whether the row is transmitted.
             Wording that conflates the two is the exact failure CLAUDE.md
             warns about, and it is worse here than in a design doc.

             Two purposes, not one. App Functionality is the sync and the feed.
             Product Personalization is `TasteMatch`: it reads another
             account's published rankings from the server and turns them into
             "what should I watch next" on their profile. The maths runs
             on-device, which changes where the work happens and not what the
             collected data is used for. Declared to App Store Connect the same
             way on 2026-08-26.

             Still absent, and each would be a new purpose here the day it
             ships: server-side recommendations, a social recommender
             ("people you follow also liked this"), and any analytics at all.
             The Discover recommender needs none of them — it runs on the local
             library plus TMDB's public vote average and reads nothing from
             Supabase. -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherUserContent</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
            <true/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
            <false/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
            <array>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeProductPersonalization</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
